1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
Hello, Kira, how are you? Fine thanks, Paul, how are you?

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,160
Well, thanks. It's good to see you

3
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:09,910
It must be twelve months since you did our course?

4
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:13,600
That's right. It's nice to come back and say hello.

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,770
What course did you enrol in?

6
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:19,750
Actually, I went straight into third year Pharmacy.

7
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:25,460
They credited me with two years, which probably made it more difficult for me.

8
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:28,460
On the other hand, you were lucky to be granted credits

9
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:30,450
Is that why you chose the course?

10
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:34,900
Yes. And, as I'd already finished a course in it in my country,

11
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:39,350
I thought it would be easier if I studied something I already knew.

12
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,600
I didn't realise you went into third year.

13
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:45,050
I thought you started in first year. No wonder it wonder it was so hard!

14
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:50,204
And what do you think is one of the big differences between studying at a university here

15
00:00:50,204 --> 00:00:51,720
and studying in your country?

16
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:55,293
Well, I've found it very difficult to write assignments,

17
00:00:55,293 --> 00:00:59,660
because I wasn't familiar with that aspect of the system here.

18
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:03,570
The main problem is that the lecturers expect you to be critical.

19
00:01:03,570 --> 00:01:09,560
That made me feel really terrible. I thought "How can I possibly do it?

20
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:12,460
How can I comment on someone else's research

21
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:15,230
when they probably spent five years doing it?"

22
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:20,885
I think lot of people who come from overseas countries have similar problems.

23
00:01:20,885 --> 00:01:24,800
But after a while it became easier for me.

24
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:29,392
People expect you to have problems with the process of reading and writing,

25
00:01:29,392 --> 00:01:34,690
but, in fact, it is more a question of altering your viewpoint towards academic study.

26
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:39,200
How was the content of the lectures? Was it easy for you?

27
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:42,890
I didn't really have many problems understanding lectures.

28
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:46,060
The content was very similar to what I'd studied before.

29
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:48,820
And what about the lecturers themselves

30
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:51,710
Are they essentially the same as lecturers in your country?

31
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:56,000
Well actually, no. Here, they're much easier to approach.

32
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,230
After every lecture you can go and ask them something you didn't understand.

33
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:04,470
Or you can make an appointment and talk to them. About anything in the course.

34
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:07,954
Maybe you found them different because you're a more mature student now,

35
00:02:07,954 --> 00:02:12,310
whereas when you were studying in your country you were younger and not so assertive.

36
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:18,060
No, I don't think that's the difference. Most of the students here do it.

37
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:21,060
In my faculty, they all seem to make appointments

38
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:25,179
usually to talk about something in the course that's worrying them,

39
00:02:25,179 --> 00:02:29,120
but sometimes just about something that might really interest them

40
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:31,230
something they might want to specialise in.

41
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:34,980
The lecturers must set aside certain times every week

42
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:36,740
when they're available for students.

43
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:42,890
That's good to hear. And how was your timetable? Was it a very busy year?

44
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:46,687
Very, very busy. They make you work very hard.

45
00:02:46,687 --> 00:02:51,750
Apart from lectures, we had practical sessions in a lot of subjects.

46
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:53,800
We did these in small groups

47
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:58,970
I had to go and work four hours every week in a community pharmacy.

48
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:03,800
Actually, I enjoyed this very much-meeting new people all the time.

49
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:08,182
Then in second semester, we had to get experience in hospital dispensaries,

50
00:03:08,182 --> 00:03:13,660
so every second day we went to one of the big hospitals and worked there.

51
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:18,770
And on top of all that we had our assignments, which took me a lot of time.

52
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:22,880
Oh, I nearly forgot, between first and second semesters

53
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:26,360
we had to work full-time for two weeks in a hospital.

54
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:28,360
That does sound a very heavy year.

55
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:32,930
So are you pleased now that you did it? Do you feel some sense of achievement?

56
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:38,930
Yeah, I do feel much more confident, which I suppose is the most important thing.

57
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:42,680
And have you got any recommendations for people who are studying from overseas?

58
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:46,150
Well, I suppose they need very good English.

59
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:47,620
It would be much better

60
00:03:47,620 --> 00:03:51,546
if they spent more time learning English before they enter the university,

61
00:03:51,546 --> 00:03:56,308
because you can be in big trouble if you don't understand what people are saying

62
00:03:56,308 --> 00:03:58,530
and you haven't got time to translate.

63
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:01,448
Anything else? Well, as I said before,

64
00:04:01,448 --> 00:04:07,700
the biggest problem for me was a lack of familiarity with the education system here.

65
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:12,630
It sounds as if it was a real challenge. Congratulations, Kira. Thanks, Paul.
